# Example .env for the ETL + viz stack. Copy to .env and fill in.
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# NOTHING here must be committed: .env is gitignored (and the real data and
# credentials stay out of git per AGENTS.md). This file only documents the
# variables the repo reads.

# --- Dev-host database (the only var the pipeline strictly needs) ------------
# The ETL (etl/config.py) and the viz app (viz/config.py) load this via
# python-dotenv from the repo-root .env. Two sane values:
#
# * dev host PostGIS (pipeline role with write rights):
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/energy_de
#
# * or the containerized db published on POSTGRES_PORT (see below):
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://etl:etl@localhost:5433/energy_de

# --- Test database (the suite's own scratch target, #12) --------------------
# The integration suite runs here, never against DATABASE_URL: it drops and
# rebuilds the pipeline schemas on every session, so pointing it at a real
# database would destroy that data. It refuses to start if both variables point
# at the same database, and with TEST_DATABASE_URL unset it cannot reach any
# database at all — the dev URL is overwritten so no test can fall back to it.
#
# Nothing needs creating up front: the suite creates the database if it is
# missing (the role needs CREATEDB), installs the PostGIS extension, and builds
# the pipeline schemas. Any throwaway database name will do.
#
# Port must match the server in DATABASE_URL above — 5433 here because the
# examples assume the containerized db; use 5432 for a dev-host PostGIS.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg2://etl:etl@localhost:5433/energy_de_test

# --- Optional: ETL data paths ------------------------------------------------
# `python -m etl extract` takes its sources folder and `python -m etl
# boundaries` its manifest from these, defaulting to the repo root (same
# anchoring as the DATABASE_URL above); an explicit TARGET argument always
# wins. Only needed when the dataset lives elsewhere.
# SOURCES_DATA_DIR=<repo>/data/sources
# BOUNDARIES_MANIFEST=<repo>/data/boundaries/boundaries.txt

# --- Optional: read-only role for the viz app ---------------------------------
# The app falls back to DATABASE_URL on the dev host, but connecting as the
# read-only viz_reader (the SQL seam provisions it — see docs/containerization.md)
# is the safe default and the path used in the container.
# VIZ_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://viz_reader:viz@localhost:5432/energy_de

# --- Optional: schema names (defaults shown; both sides must agree) -----------
# The database schemas the ETL writes/reads and the viz app reads.  Resolved by
# etl/config.py (pipeline) and viz/config.py (app) with these defaults — only
# override for a custom layout, and only on both sides at once.
# RAW_SCHEMA=raw
# STAGING_SCHEMA=stage
# CORE_SCHEMA=core
# SERVICE_SCHEMA=service
# MARTS_SCHEMA=marts

# --- Compose / seed / smoke knobs (read by scripts/*.sh + compose.yaml) -------
# Only needed to override the documented defaults; the defaults are shown to
# the right as reminders.
# ETL_DATA_VOLUME=etl_data          # detached data volume (data/ + docker.env; local variant only)
# POSTGRES_PORT=5433                # host port for the db service (local variant only)
# VIZ_PORT=8501                     # host port for the Streamlit viz service
# NGINX_PORT=80                     # host port for the nginx proxy (server compose.yaml)
# PIPELINE_IMAGE=khvostenko/aws-energy-etl:latest   # pipeline image compose.yaml pulls (Docker Hub)
# VIZ_IMAGE=khvostenko/aws-energy-viz:latest        # viz image compose.yaml pulls (Docker Hub)
# VIZ_USER=viz_reader               # read-only role the app connects as
# VIZ_PASSWORD=viz                  # must match docker/viz_reader.sql (coupled pair)
# DB_HOST=db                        # seed: host written into docker.env
# DB_PORT=5432
# DB_USER=etl
# DB_PASSWORD=etl
# DB_NAME=energy_de

# --- Server deployment (compose.yaml / build_compose.yaml, issue #8) ---------
# The server stack's pipeline container runs `python -m etl startup`: it
# bootstraps the database preconditions, checks the fixed S3 keys, applies the
# current Boundary releases, enqueues the current Source object versions, and
# then starts the ingestion worker. These settings come from the environment
# (the compose files declare them required); the pipeline container mounts no
# data volume — the worker reads S3 through the event queue.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@db:5432/energy_de   # pipeline role
# S3_BUCKET=energy-data          # versioned bucket: boundaries/ + sources/ keys
# SQS_QUEUE_URL=https://...      # queue carrying the S3 ObjectCreated events
# SNS_TOPIC_ARN=arn:aws:sns:...  # topic for rejected-file, blocked-startup and
#                                 # DLQ alerts
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=eu-central-1 # region the bucket, queue and topic live in
# VIZ_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://viz_reader:viz@db:5432/energy_de  # read-only role
#
# Recovery: `docker compose run --rm pipeline python -m etl redrive` re-enqueues
# failed or DLQ object versions (--key narrows to one key). See
# docs/containerization.md ("Server startup path") and docs/remote-deploy.md.